
# Stray compiler probe output (see fuzz work, 2026-06)
rust_out
target
.claude/worktrees

# Cargo.lock files are TRACKED, deliberately. Two reasons:
#   1. An application-side workspace pins its dependencies. `app/src-tauri` is
#      a shipped binary, not a library, and a shipped binary must build from
#      the same resolved versions every time.
#   2. The SOUP inventory of a class C unit cites its lockfile as the evidence
#      for every version it records (IEC 62304 §8.1.2). An untracked lockfile
#      makes that evidence unreproducible.
# See org-members/docs/architecture/soup.md.

# Guardrails: worktrees and sed backups (managed by /ratchet)
.worktrees/
*.bak

# Local tool output. Untracked-but-not-ignored directories read as
# UNCLAIMED-PATH to check-units.sh, which scans `git ls-files --others`.
.claire/
_apalache-out/

# proptest failure seeds are NEVER committed here. This project's verification
# method deliberately breaks production code to prove a test discriminates
# (the mutation protocol: apply a mutation, watch it red, revert). A proptest
# run under a mutation writes the failing input to `*.proptest-regressions`,
# and that seed is a seed for behaviour the software never had. Committed, it
# would be replayed on every later run and pin the suite to the broken
# version. The cost of losing a genuine regression seed is one rediscovery;
# the cost of committing a mutation seed is a permanently misleading suite.
*.proptest-regressions
